import {strengthenReading} from './reading-transfer.js?v=20261029-reading-v2';
export const source='https://www.aqa.org.uk/subjects/english/gcse/english-8700/specification';
export const specIssue='AQA8700 checked29October2026; 2026 question changes and announced2027 layout changes checked';
export const topics=[{id:'english-language.reading',title:'Reading foundations',unit:'8700-reading'}];
export const units=[{id:'8700-reading',title:'AQA GCSE English Language',paper:'Paper 1 · reading foundations'}];
// Original teaching passages, not quotations from a named writer or official examination sources.
const units01=[
['Reading what is actually there',`At 5:12, the station clock stopped. Mina checked her watch and waited beside the closed gate. A porter carried a ladder across the platform. He set it below the clock, then went back for his tools. Mina put her ticket into her coat pocket. Beyond the gate, the rails shone in the rain.`, 'The clock has stopped at 5:12.', 'the closed gate', 'The gate prevents entry at this moment.', 'It does not establish that the whole station has closed permanently.', 'A porter carries a ladder to the clock.', 'Select two explicit details about Mina’s situation; explain what each does and does not establish.'],
['Inference with a firm footing',`Jules left muddy boots by the door. On the kitchen table, two cups stood beside a bottle of water. He lifted one cup, then put it down without drinking. His hand left a small dark circle on the wood. When a car slowed outside, he turned before the engine stopped.`, 'Two cups are on the table.', 'put it down without drinking', 'The interrupted action can suggest distraction or unease.', 'The text does not identify a bereavement or explain the entire cause of his feelings.', 'He reacts before the engine stops.', 'Infer Jules’s state of mind using two actions. Qualify a possible alternative and avoid inventing a cause.'],
['Vocabulary belongs to its context',`The applause was thin. It began in the front row, faltered, and returned from somewhere near the back. Sal kept her hands flat against the lectern. The speech she had practised in the mirror now lay in a folded square beside the microphone. She thanked the audience twice.`, 'Sal is standing at a lectern.', 'thin', 'The scattered, hesitant applause makes thin suggest limited or weak support.', 'Thin does not describe the physical thickness of the sound.', 'The applause falters before returning.', 'Explain thin in context, then connect the surrounding sequence to Sal’s response.'],
['Choosing the reference that proves the point',`The hall bell rang, but nobody rose. A chair scraped once and fell silent. Nia leaned towards her brother and whispered that they should wait. At the far end of the room, a custodian checked the ceiling. A narrow line of water travelled down the wall beside him.`, 'Water is travelling down a wall.', 'whispered that they should wait', 'The quiet instruction supports a claim that Nia is cautious about leaving.', 'The bell alone does not prove why everyone stays seated.', 'A chair scrapes and then falls silent.', 'Support the claim that the group is waiting cautiously. Choose a precise reference and explain its relevance.'],
['Paraphrase without changing the evidence',`Halfway across the footbridge, Amir stopped. The boards moved beneath his shoes when a cyclist passed. He gripped the rail until the bicycle reached the opposite bank. Then he loosened one hand and took a single step. Under the bridge, the river carried leaves towards the bend.`, 'Amir stops halfway across a footbridge.', 'loosened one hand and took a single step', 'A paraphrase should preserve the gradual, limited movement.', 'Saying that he races fearlessly across adds speed and certainty absent from the passage.', 'He holds the rail while the cyclist passes.', 'Paraphrase the final movement accurately; then add analysis of how its small scale shapes our view of Amir.'],
['A narrator is a viewpoint, not the author',`I recognised the handwriting before I opened the letter. Across the table, Ruth watched my thumb slide under the flap. She asked whether it was good news. I said I had not read it yet and kept the writing turned towards me. The paper crackled so loudly that I wished she would look away.`, 'The narrator recognises the handwriting.', 'I wished she would look away', 'The first-person account directly reveals this narrator’s discomfort.', 'It does not give direct access to Ruth’s private thoughts or prove an event happened to the author.', 'Ruth asks whether the letter contains good news.', 'Explain how first-person perspective shapes the scene, including one limit on what the reader can know.'],
['Sensory details create a particular atmosphere',`Warm bread scented the lane behind the bakery. Len paused beneath a window where trays cooled on a rack. Further along, the sharp smell of smoke caught in his throat. The baker stepped outside and pointed towards a bin against the wall. Len moved his bicycle away from it.`, 'Trays are cooling on a rack.', 'sharp smell of smoke', 'The unpleasant sensation interrupts the earlier comfort of warm bread.', 'Smoke does not by itself prove that a house or the whole bakery is on fire.', 'Len moves his bicycle after the baker points.', 'Trace the sensory contrast and explain how the interruption changes the atmosphere without exaggerating the danger.'],
['A comparison is not a literal report',`Fog folded the street like a wet cloth. Windows that had shone a moment ago became dull squares. Priya could still see the kerb beside her shoes, but the crossing light had disappeared. She waited for its familiar sound before stepping forward. Behind her, a shop door closed.`, 'Priya can still see the nearby kerb.', 'like a wet cloth', 'The simile suggests concealment and a heavy, damp enclosure.', 'The street is not literally wrapped in fabric, and the text does not say that Priya is lost.', 'She waits for a familiar crossing sound.', 'Analyse the wet-cloth comparison and explain how a nearby detail limits a claim of complete helplessness.'],
['Verbs shape movement and agency',`The wind clawed at the loose gate. Each gust pulled it open a little, then slammed it against the post. Dan reached for the latch and missed as the metal rattled against his sleeve. He planted one boot against the bottom rail before trying again. This time, the bolt slid into place.`, 'The gate is loose.', 'clawed', 'The forceful verb gives the wind an aggressive, almost animal-like quality.', 'It does not turn the wind into a literal creature or remove Dan’s ability to act.', 'Dan braces the gate before fastening the bolt.', 'Explain the effect of clawed, then show how Dan’s actions complicate a reading of human powerlessness.'],
['Sentence movement needs a local explanation',`Run. The instruction arrived from the far side of the field. Eva took three strides, stopped beside the wall and listened while the distant voices spread out towards the road. Nothing moved behind her. She waited until she could distinguish her own breathing from the hiss of the trees.`, 'Eva stops beside a wall.', 'Run.', 'The isolated imperative introduces abrupt urgency before a longer sentence slows attention to listening.', 'A short sentence does not always create tension in every text; its position and contrast matter here.', 'Eva waits and listens after stopping.', 'Analyse the contrast between the opening command and the later listening. Refer to sentence movement and context.'],
['An opening directs the first questions',`The clock above the theatre door ticked loudly enough for Noor to hear it from the pavement. Every poster advertised the same performance. Inside, the ticket desk was empty. She checked the date once more, then tried the handle. From behind the curtain came a single cough.`, 'The ticket desk is empty.', 'The clock above the theatre door', 'Opening with the audible clock draws attention to waiting and time before the interior is explored.', 'The passage has not yet confirmed that the performance is cancelled.', 'A cough is heard behind the curtain at the end.', 'Explain how the opening and final sound guide a reader’s questions about the apparently empty theatre.'],
['Following a shift of focus',`From the hill, the market looked like a patchwork of coloured roofs. Down among the stalls, people moved in narrow channels between fruit crates. A child counted coins beside a basket. One silver piece balanced on her thumbnail before dropping into the seller’s palm. The seller nodded and handed her an orange.`, 'The scene begins with a view from a hill.', 'One silver piece balanced on her thumbnail', 'Attention narrows from a broad market view to a small transaction and a precise movement.', 'This is a description of narrative focus, not proof that a physical camera is filming the scene.', 'The seller gives the child an orange.', 'Trace the narrowing focus through three points and explain why the final exchange becomes significant.'],
['Withholding and revealing information',`Tessa kept the envelope under her sleeve as she crossed the square. At the noticeboard, she waited for the two men beside it to move away. She took out the paper and smoothed its corners. It was a request for someone to look after a lost dog. She pinned it where the rain could not reach.`, 'Tessa carries an envelope under her sleeve.', 'It was a request for someone to look after a lost dog.', 'The delayed explanation can make an initially secretive-looking action appear ordinary and considerate.', 'The opening behaviour does not establish criminal intent or a dangerous message.', 'She places the notice out of the rain.', 'Explain how the delayed revelation changes a possible first impression; distinguish curiosity from confirmed danger.'],
['An ending can reframe the opening',`At dusk, the tower windows were black. Mara climbed the stairs with a torch tucked under one arm. On the landing she found the switch she remembered from childhood. Nothing happened when she pressed it. At the top, she lit a small lamp, and one square of gold appeared above the harbour.`, 'Mara climbs the tower at dusk.', 'one square of gold appeared', 'The ending changes the initial darkness through a small, local act of illumination.', 'It does not prove that the entire harbour or every problem in Mara’s life has been transformed.', 'The remembered switch does not work.', 'Connect the ending to the opening and qualify the scale of the change.'],
['Language detail or whole-text structure?',`Someone called Ada’s name from the platform. She continued to load the heavy bags onto the cart. Wheels rattled past her along the wet stones. At the entrance she stopped to move a fallen sign out of the way. Someone called her name again. This time, she turned.`, 'Ada loads bags onto a cart.', 'Someone called her name again.', 'The repeated call frames intervening actions; the changed response gives the return a structural role.', 'Calling heavy an adjective alone does not analyse this passage’s organisation.', 'Ada turns only after the repeated call.', 'Distinguish one language observation from a structural pattern and explain the changed response.'],
['A judgment needs a test',`The library door stood open. Inside, a volunteer smiled at Malik and pointed to a chair. He set down his bag beside a table of free books. At the desk, a notice said that only registered members could use the computers. Malik read it twice before sitting down.`, 'Free books are on a table.', 'only registered members could use the computers', 'The restriction qualifies a reading of unrestricted welcome, alongside the open door and smile.', 'One restriction does not prove that every person is unwelcome.', 'The volunteer smiles and points towards a chair.', 'Evaluate the view that the library is presented as completely welcoming. Weigh support and a qualification.'],
['Counter-evidence strengthens a qualified view',`Owen stepped into the boat without hesitation. He checked the rope twice and placed a spare coat beneath the seat. When the engine started, he grinned at the other passengers. At the harbour mouth he slowed down and watched the water beyond the wall. Only then did he turn towards the open sea.`, 'Owen checks the rope twice.', 'slowed down and watched the water', 'The precaution qualifies a claim of recklessness without proving that he lacks confidence.', 'Confidence and care are not opposites; neither a grin nor a precaution establishes every motive.', 'He enters the boat without hesitation.', 'Evaluate a claim that Owen is entirely reckless, using evidence that both invites and limits that view.'],
['Testing two possible readings',`The classroom was quiet after the bell. Rosa arranged three pencils in a straight line beside her notebook. Her friend pulled out the next chair but did not sit. They both watched the closed door. When its handle moved, Rosa breathed out slowly and uncapped her pen.`, 'Rosa arranges three pencils.', 'breathed out slowly', 'The controlled action may suggest relief or preparation; the watching and closed door can support unease.', 'Neither calm nor anxiety is a complete diagnosis, and the visitor’s identity is not supplied.', 'Both students watch the closed door.', 'Compare two plausible readings of Rosa’s behaviour, then explain which is better supported and what remains uncertain.'],
['Building a paragraph that does analytical work',`The photograph had faded to a pale rectangle. Isaac held it by the edges, careful not to touch the two faces. He tilted it towards the window, but the light only showed a crease through the centre. His fingers shook once. Then he set it face down beside an empty frame.`, 'Isaac holds the photograph by its edges.', 'His fingers shook once.', 'A brief loss of control can suggest emotion, while the careful handling gives the object personal significance.', 'The scene does not confirm who the faces belong to or explain a specific death or quarrel.', 'He sets the photograph face down beside an empty frame.', 'Write a paragraph connecting careful handling and the final action to a qualified reading of the photograph’s significance.'],
['Independent reading: apply and review',`A loose gate knocked against the garden wall. Bea put down her basket and tried the latch. Rust stained her palm. She wiped it on a leaf, pushed the gate firmly into its frame and lifted the bolt. Behind her, the first apples rolled from the tilted basket onto the path.`, 'Bea puts down her basket.', 'Rust stained her palm.', 'The small physical detail makes the repair awkward and tactile rather than effortless.', 'The passage does not make Bea’s whole life or personality certain from one action.', 'Apples roll from the tilted basket while she repairs the gate.', 'Practise distinguishing observation, inference and method here; then attempt the different passage in independent writing.']
];
const item=(id,stage,n,prompt,correct,d1,d2,why,concept)=>{const answer=(n+id.length)%3,options=[d1,d2];options.splice(answer,0,correct);return{id:`${id}.${stage}.${n}`,prompt,options,answer,why,concept:`${id}.${concept}`};};
export const lessons=units01.map(([title,text,fact,detail,reading,limit,second,writing],i)=>{
 const id=`lang.reading.lesson-${String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}`,refs=['3.1.1',i<6?'AO1':i<15?'AO2':'AO4'];
 const words=[[detail,`${reading} ${limit}`],['Evidence and sequence',`${fact} ${second} Locate these observations before developing an interpretation; inference must remain accountable to the words.`],['A qualified judgment',`${limit} A strong response can explain a likely effect while acknowledging what the passage leaves unresolved.`]];
 const q=(s,n,...args)=>item(id,s,n,...args);
 return{id,topicId:'english-language.reading',title,scope:`Original passage ${i+1} · ${title.toLowerCase()}`,minutes:30,refs,author:'Crash Course',rights:'original_teaching',textType:'prose',textTitle:`Original practice passage ${i+1}`,checked:'29 October 2026',sources:[source+'/subject-content/scope-of-study',source+'/scheme-of-assessment','https://www.aqa.org.uk/english-language-changes'],contextReading:[{label:'AQA scope of study',url:source+'/subject-content/scope-of-study'},{label:'AQA assessment objectives',url:source+'/scheme-of-assessment'},{label:'2026 question changes',url:'https://www.aqa.org.uk/english-language-changes'}],sourceNote:'Passage and all teaching/questions are original Crash Course practice, not a quotation from a named writer, historical document or official examination. These short passages introduce reading skills; authentic established-writer texts, period/genre range and longer independent assessment remain to build. AQA 2026 updates checked;2027 source-layout announcement noted. Writing is unmarked and specialist review pending.',explorerNote:'Original teaching passage · not an official exam source. Read the complete passage and test interpretations against it.',
 cards:[['Locate before interpreting',`Read the complete passage without trying to name every technique. ${fact} ${second} Separate these observations from a possible explanation of a character’s feelings. Re-read the immediate context when a claim depends on one word, pronoun or action. A plausible explanation still needs a reference.`,],['Explain this particular choice',`${reading} The detail “${detail}” must be explained through its position in this passage. Explain what changes in attention, understanding or expectation; naming a device or repeating the quotation is only a starting point. Connect the wording to your interpretation rather than attaching a universal effect.`,],['Qualify and construct an answer',`${limit} Build a supported response by making a focused claim, choosing a precise reference and explaining its contribution. If another detail complicates the first impression, use it to refine the judgment. A qualification is not indecision when it distinguishes evidence from speculation.`,]],
 views:[{kind:'language',label:'Read and annotate',title:'Original practice passage',content:text,readingRegion:true,words},{kind:'people',label:'Test readings',title:'Evidence or overreach?',rows:[['Supported observation',fact],['Supported interpretation',reading],['Boundary',limit]]},{kind:'timeline',label:'Build an explanation',title:'From observation to judgment',events:[['Locate',fact],['Select',detail],['Explain',reading],['Qualify',limit]]}],
 tasks:[[`Which reading fits “${detail}” in this passage?`,[reading,'The detail proves every private motive and later event.','The passage has no meaning until every device is named.'],0,'A supported reading relates a precise choice to the local context.','Re-read the detail and neighbouring actions; avoid turning a possibility into an unsupported certainty.']],
 practice:[q('practice',1,`What is explicit in passage ${i+1}?`,fact,'The author confirms every character’s private motive.','Every event is a verified historical record.','An explicit observation is stated or directly shown; it is not a complete explanation of motive.','evidence'),q('practice',2,`What can “${detail}” contribute here?`,reading,'It guarantees one universal reader reaction in every context.','It proves the writer experienced this event personally.','Connect this wording or position to the scene; a literary possibility is not a biographical fact.','method'),q('practice',3,`Which qualification keeps your answer proportionate?`,limit,'No interpretation can ever be supported.','A longer answer automatically makes an inference true.','A useful limit identifies what the evidence does not establish without discarding a supported interpretation.','judgment')],
 check:[q('check',1,`Which second observation is supported in passage ${i+1}?`,second,'A narrator knows every person’s unspoken thoughts.','Every surrounding event has been explained.','Check what is actually presented rather than supplying missing circumstances.','evidence'),q('check',2,`Which claim is best supported by “${detail}”?`,reading,'The words must be interpreted literally in every case.','The passage confirms a complete life story.','A precise interpretation connects the local evidence with a proportionate explanation.','method'),q('check',3,`What would overstate the evidence in this passage?`,'Claim that a suggested feeling proves a complete private history.','Use a short relevant reference.','Explain a change of focus with evidence.','An inference about a moment does not establish an entire biography or a guaranteed cause.','limits'),q('check',4,`How should your response to ${title.toLowerCase()} develop?`,'Connect a relevant reference to a reasoned claim and check it against another detail.','List terminology without explaining the passage.','Replace analysis with a claim that all readers agree.','The argument needs evidence, explanation and an appropriate boundary on the judgment.','argument')],writing,rubric:[`Use accurate evidence from the complete supplied passage, including “${detail}” where relevant.`,`Explain how the particular choice or sequence supports an interpretation: ${reading}`,`Qualify what is uncertain and organise a coherent response; ${limit}`],...(i===19?{independentWriting:true,independentTaskType:'reading-response',independentTaskLabel:'Fresh original reading response, not an official paper or automatically marked assessment.',independentPrompt:'How does the writer present a difficult decision in the passage? Analyse language and the movement of attention, then evaluate the view that the character appears completely in control. Support and qualify your judgment.',independentExtractLabel:'Fresh original Crash Course passage · The harbour light',independentExtract:'The harbour light blinked twice and went dark. On the pier, Imani held the rope while the small boat rose beside her. The nearest lamp showed the frayed fibres rubbing against the ring. She glanced back towards the road, where a car had stopped without turning off its headlights. Imani called for help. Before anyone answered, she wrapped the rope around the post and stepped away from the edge. The boat struck the stone once, then settled against its fender.',independentIntro:'Aim for about 25 minutes including planning and checking; timing is advisory, not enforced. This is a new short original practice text, not a full-length official paper. If already familiar, describe the attempt as revision. First response and later revisions are saved separately.'}: {})};
});
lessons[19].rubric=['Use accurate references to the fresh harbour passage; distinguish what is stated from an inference.','Explain a particular language choice and how attention moves between the rope, the road and Imani’s actions.','Evaluate complete control using both the frayed rope or call for help and Imani’s practical response; qualify what remains uncertain.'];
strengthenReading(lessons);
export const course={lessons,topics,units,source,specIssue,key:'crash-course-language-v1',subjectId:'english-language',subjectName:'English Language',specCode:'8700',allowAI:false,scopeNote:'20 built Language lessons of 175 planned. Knowledge completion is separate from unmarked written responses. Authentic text range, remaining 155 lessons, spoken performance and specialist review remain outstanding.'};
